4. Global efforts to reduce greenhouse gas emissions
International efforts to reduce GHG emissions have involved several significant initiatives and agreements. The Kyoto Protocol, adopted in 1997, imposed commitments on developed countries to reduce GHG emissions. However, due to its limited scope and non-participation by developing countries, this approach proved to be ineffective (Lau et al., 2012). In response to these limitations, the Paris Agreement was reached in 2015, providing a comprehensive framework for participating countries to set national GHG reduction targets. It acknowledged the special responsibility of developed countries and provided financial support for developing nations. Under the Paris Agreement, countries agreed to take measures to limit global warming to “well below” 2 ◦C above pre-industrial levels (Rogelj et al., 2016). Considering the potential impact on climate and ecosystems, there was also a commitment to strive for efforts to limit warming to 1.5 ◦C. These temperature goals underscore the seriousness of commitments to reducing GHG emissions and each country's contribution to global efforts to combat climate change. However, the current concentration of GHG is projected to lead to temperature increases by the end of this century significantly above the targets set by the Paris Agreement, accompanied by more extreme weather conditions. Additionally, in this context, international financial mechanisms play a crucial role, such as the Green Climate Fund for developing countries, established to finance projects aimed at reducing GHG emissions and adapting to climate change (Hitz and Smith, 2004; Van Kerkhoff et al., 2011; Adger et al., 2013; Antimiani et al., 2017; Dean et al., 2018; Busch et al., 2022; Chien et al., 2023). Furthermore, regional and bilateral agreements complement these efforts, providing more targeted and adapted emission reduction strategies, as well as facilitating the exchange of experiences and technologies among countries. These agreements, therefore, serve as important instruments in the global effort to mitigate the impact of GHG and ensure a sustainable future for the planet. Efforts to reduce GHG emissions involve diverse strategies and measures at the national government and global community levels, aimed at transitioning to more sustainable methods of energy production and reducing the adverse effects on the Earth's climate system. International initiatives to reduce GHG emissions find practical embodiment in the strategies of various countries that successfully implement innovative approaches to sustainable energy. One example of a successful case is Sweden that actively integrates renewable sources such as wind and hydroenergy, and incentivises the purchase of electric vehicles, challenging the necessity of relying on fossil fuels (Zhong et al., 2021a, 2021b). Costa Rica, fully embracing renewable energy sources, including hydroelectric power, solar, and wind installations, serves as a shining example of a country that has fully transitioned to clean energy (Godínez-Zamora et al., 2020). Denmark, focusing efforts on wind energy and innovation, has achieved impressive results in creating a sustainable energy system (Korberg et al., 2020). Key features of its approach include significant investments in wind energy, the development of energy-efficient technologies, and the stimulation of energy consumption from renewable sources. These countries serve as vivid examples of successful strategies in reducing emissions in line with the commitments outlined in the Paris Agreement. Their experiences underscore the importance of global cooperation, investment in sustainable technologies, and the commitment to the shared goal of creating a sustainable future for our planet.
These successful initiatives provide undeniable evidence that transitioning to sustainable methods of production and consumption benefits not only the environment but also the economy and society as a whole. Global efforts to reduce GHG emissions require a better understanding of the problem and collaboration and knowledge exchange among countries and organizations. This path not only creates favourable conditions for future generations but also contributes to economic growth, improves public health, and ensures global sustainability (Nordahl et al., 2020). The responsibility for our future lies in global efforts aimed at preserving our planet and ensuring a sustainable existence for all.
5. Primary greenhouse gases
GHG, such as CO2, CH4, and N2O, are capable of capturing and retaining thermal radiation in the Earth's atmosphere. They play a primary role in the greenhouse effect. CO2, CH4, and N2O retain heat due to their ability to absorb and emit thermal infrared radiation. This process is associated with the specific molecular structure of these gases. CO2 has a linear arrangement between a single carbon and two oxygen atoms. This dipole structure facilitates an interaction with infrared radiation (Galashev, 2011; Kirk-Davidoff, 2018). CO2 also has a long atmospheric lifetime of 50 to 200 years (Inman, 2008). It absorbs and emits thermal radiation in specific wavelength ranges (Harries et al., 2008). When thermal infrared radiation enters the atmosphere, CO2 molecules absorb this energy and re-emit it in various directions, including back to the Earth's surface. This process contributes to the retention of heat in the atmosphere. N2O, composed of two nitrogen (N) atoms and one oxygen (O) atom, also has a linear structure. A relatively long-lived gas at about 114 years (IPCC, 2001), it can also emit infrared radiation. While its contribution to the greenhouse effect is not as significant as either CO2 or CH4 (Tian et al., 2011), it plays a crucial role in atmospheric chemistry and affects the climate through aerosol particle formation, influencing cloud properties, chemical reactions, and the atmospheric heat balance. Additionally, N2O impacts ozone levels and regulates methane concentrations that also influences climate (Ravishankara et al., 2009). The climate impact of N2O is complex and dependent on various factors, including atmospheric conditions and geographical location. In contrast to the linear structures of CO2 and N2O, CH4 consists of one carbon (C) atom and four hydrogen (H) atoms in a tetrahedral arrangement. It is a more efficient greenhouse gas compared to CO2 (Jeffry et al., 2021) and has a shorter atmospheric lifetime of only about 12 years (EPA, 2023). Its molecular structure allows it to both absorb and emit infrared radiation, effectively trapping heat and making it a significant component of the greenhouse effect. Only about 45 % of CO2 that is emitted remains in the atmosphere (Raupach et al., 2014). The remainder is absorbed: 25 % by the ocean (Watson et al., 2020), and approximately 30 % by terrestrial ecosystems such as forests (Duffy et al., 2021). This balance can vary from year-toyear due to natural climate cycles and other factors (Peters et al., 2017). Taking into account the heat-trapping characteristics measured in terms of global warming potential, CH4 is 23 times more effective, and N2O is 296 times more effective than CO2 (IPCC, 2001; Liu and Greaver, 2009). However, the amount of CO2 in the Earth's atmosphere significantly exceeds the amounts of CH4 or N2O making it of much greater concern. The continued accumulation of CO2 in the atmosphere makes it the most troubling of the three major GHGs. The problem will exacerbate with continued emissions. Considering the long lifespan of CO2 as a GHG, the achieved temperature level will persist for several decades even if emissions were reduced to zero. While these three GHGs play a crucial role in maintaining the Earth's warmth, an excess of these gases, as a result of human activities, leads to the intensification of the greenhouse effect, climate change, and global warming.
