6. Greenhouse gas sources
The primary source of CO2 is the combustion of fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and natural gas. Compounding the effect of CO2 emissions, deforestation is a significant factor in carbon dioxide absorption as trees can sequester a significant amount of CO2 (Koerner and Klopatek, 2002; Raupach et al., 2007; Ho et al., 2011; Raihan and Tuspekova, 2022). Anthropogenic sources of CH4 include activities related to the extraction and transportation of natural gas, organic waste processing in landfills, and livestock farming (Ramachandra et al., 2015; Gong and Shi, 2021; Shahzad et al., 2021). N2O emissions occur in agriculture, industrial processes, and fuel combustion (Smith, 2017; Tian et al., 2020; JovaniSancho et al., 2023), with fertilizers used in agriculture being a major contributor to these emissions. Water vapor and ozone also contribute, regulated by natural processes and anthropogenic activities. Human activities significantly increase the concentration of GHG in the atmosphere, intensifying the greenhouse effect and impacting the climate (Althor et al., 2016; Zheng et al., 2019). According to data from Table 1, provided by the Emission Database for Global Atmospheric Research (EDGAR) and developed by the European Commission's Joint Research Centre (Crippa et al., 2018), the main sources of these emissions are concentrated in several key sectors, each contributing differently to the overall GHG gas emissions. The power industry is a leading source, mainly from the burning of fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and natural gas for electricity generation (Lamb et al., 2021; Liu et al., 2023). Industrial combustion in sectors like chemicals, steel, and cement also increases GHG emissions, with CO2 resulting from fuel combustion and chemical reactions, along with CH4 and N2O from various processes
(Hong et al., 2021). Transportation contributes to emissions through the combustion of gasoline, diesel, and kerosene in cars, airplanes, and ships, releasing CO2, N2O, and CH4. Agriculture generates GHG from rice cultivation, livestock farming, and fertilizer use, with significant emissions of CH4 and N2O (Nayak et al., 2015; Kim et al., 2016; Menegat et al., 2022). The waste sector produces GHG through the decomposition of organic waste and its incineration, while buildings emit CO2 due to energy use and CH4 from natural gas-based heating systems (Zhong et al., 2021a, 2021b). Industrial processes and fuel exploitation, including oil and gas extraction, add to these emissions (Malik et al., 2016; Grasso, 2019). These diverse sources emphasize the need to implement strategies to reduce GHG emissions to combat climate change and promote sustainable development. Understanding the contributions from each sector, as illustrated in Table 1 provided by EDGAR, is crucial for developing effective mitigation measures to address this global challenge. Aquatic ecosystems, especially in tropical regions, play an important role in the emission of GHGs, such as CO2 and CH4 (Dalal and Allen, 2008; Teodoru et al., 2015; Kumar et al., 2020; Hui et al., 2022; Deng et al., 2023). These emissions result from natural processes like organic matter decomposition and anaerobic fermentation, as well as from anthropogenic impacts. In tropical zones, where temperature and biological activity are higher, the decomposition of organic matter is accelerated, leading to increased CO2 emissions. Wetlands and other water-rich ecosystems common in the tropics release CH4 due to methanogenesis, which occurs under low oxygen conditions. Furthermore, human factors like deforestation, land use changes, and runoff from agricultural and industrial waste can also boost GHG emissions from aquatic ecosystems (Ran et al., 2021). For instance, nutrient pollution from agricultural runoff can lead to eutrophication, which can increase CO2 and CH4 emissions (Bhattacharyya et al., 2022; Chan et al., 2024). Thus, the impact of aquatic ecosystems on global GHG emissions is significant, especially in tropical regions, and should be taken into account when developing strategies to combat climate change. Fig. 1 represents GHG emissions in gigatons of carbon dioxide equivalents (GtCO2eq), reflecting the contributions of various sectors to overall pollution levels. EDGAR data shows that the power industry is the single greatest contributor with 14.67 GtCO2eq or 38.1 % of overall emissions. Transport, including car emissions primarily from larger vehicles, at 7.97 GtCO2eq or 20.7 % of the total, also plays a crucial role in climate change. Industrial combustion and processes contribute 6.54 (17 %) and 3.23 GtCO2eq (8.4 %), respectively, emphasizing the
