019). Addressing this issue requires concerted efforts and global strategies to reduce dependence on fossil resources and transition to sustainable practices and technologies.
7. Forests as a key factor in mitigating climate change
The rise in GHG is not only a result of increased emissions, it is also caused by less CO2 being sequestered by plants. Forests, covering approximately 30 % of the Earth's surface, play a key role in mitigating climate change, as trees absorb carbon from the air during their growth (Trumbore et al., 2015). A recent study (Harris et al., 2021) revealed that forests absorb approximately 7.6 GtCO2eq, which is nearly 1.5 times the annual CO2 emissions of the United States (4.8 GtCO2eq) and four times more than Russia (1.9 GtCO2eq). Tropical forests, mainly located in South America (Amazon), Africa (Congo Basin), and Southeast Asia, absorb more CO2 than boreal forests in Canada and Siberia (Schimel et al., 2015; Artaxo et al., 2022; Chen et al., 2024). However, it's worth noting that this figure may vary from year-to-year and can be subject to changes due to various factors, including land use, forest fires,
climate change, etc. Tropical forests surpass boreal forests in carbon sequestration due to high plant biodiversity, rapid plant growth, high biomass density, and soil microorganism activity (Lejeune et al., 2018; Tagesson et al., 2020). The warm and humid tropical climate provides constant conditions for intensive plant growth and efficient photosynthetic activity (Lewis et al., 2009; Osland et al., 2021). High biomass density and soil microorganism activity contribute to maximum carbon absorption and retention in the ecosystem, making tropical forests particularly significant in the context of climate change mitigation (Arnell et al., 2016; Pecl et al., 2017; Mitchard, 2018; Mora et al., 2018; Cook-Patton et al., 2020; Baldrian et al., 2023). In contrast, boreal forests are characterized by lower temperatures and slower carbon turnover with only a 3 to 4 month growing season, reducing the ability to absorb CO2. Therefore, preserving tropical forests in their pristine state can contribute to additional temperature reduction within the framework of global warming. Unlike other sectors where CO2 is emitted into the atmosphere in one direction, forests act as a two-way transport flow. They absorb CO2 during their growth or regrowth and release it when cut down or degraded. However, human-induced deforestation over the last 8000 years has led to significant carbon emissions. This process can be offset through afforestation (planting forests in new areas), reforestation (planting trees in cleared areas), and natural forest regeneration, which has great potential for retaining carbon in plants and soil. Combining these approaches can be key to successfully combating climate change.
8. Conclusions
Several important conclusions can be made from a comprehensive review of GHG, the sources of emissions, carbon sequestration, climate impact, and the on-going global effort to reduce emissions. First and foremost, it is clear that of GHGs such as CO2, CH4, and N2O are closely tied to climate change. The primary sources of these gases include the burning of fossil fuels, industrial processes, and to a much lesser extent, agriculture. Deforestation also plays a role by reducing the amount of carbon sequestered in vegetation. Emphasis must be placed on adopting a systematic approach to emissions reduction, covering a wide range of measures across different sectors of the economy. The consequences of climate change pose serious challenges to the global community. Extreme weather conditions, rising sea levels, ecosystem stress, threats to agriculture, and human health all demand urgent measures to mitigate their effects. International efforts to reduce emissions, particularly the Kyoto Protocol and the Paris Agreement, play a crucial role in forming a collective front against climate change. However, there is a need for more effective strategies and coordinated actions, especially considering that current emissions of GHGs exceeds established targets. Financial support for developing countries provided by international mechanisms such as the Green Climate Fund plays a key role in achieving global emission reduction goals. Nevertheless, the focus is on the need for additional resources and strategies to achieve ambitious goals, such as keeping global warming “significantly below” 2 ◦C compared to pre-industrial levels. This review underscores that successful emission reduction strategies are already being implemented in several countries, such as Sweden, Costa Rica, and Denmark. These positive examples demonstrate that a transition to sustainable methods of production and consumption is possible and brings benefits not only to the environment but also to the economy and society as a whole. Further collective efforts are needed on a global scale to create a sustainable future. Global initiatives, international agreements, effective strategies, and the exchange of experiences – all these elements are an integral part of the response to the challenge of climate change. The universal responsibility for preserving the planet lies in the power of unity, coordination, and the adoption of balanced measures towards a sustainable future.
